{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_48","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"git status --short && git diff --check && sed -n '1,120p' cores/bench/implementation_notes.md\"","aggregated_output":" M cores/bench/rtl/core.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/core_pkg.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/ex_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/id_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/if_stage.sv\n# Implementation notes\n\n- Added a 16-entry direct-mapped JALR target buffer in IF. It uses the\n  fetch PC bits `[5:2]` as the index, stores the full PC tag, and predicts\n  only valid, aligned targets for recognized JALR instructions.\n- Carried the hit flag and predicted target through IF/ID and ID/EX. EX\n  redirects on a miss or target mismatch. If a predicted target resolves\n  as misaligned, EX redirects back to `pc + 4` while preserving the existing\n  misalignment trap behavior.\n- Deviation: the resolved PC and JALR target are captured in EX/MEM and\n  written into the table from those registered values one cycle later. The\n  timing report showed the table write address on the longest path, so I\n  staged the update through existing EX/MEM fields. This raised the option-0\n  Fmax from 42.085 to 42.404 MHz across the two local runs, while increasing\n  the reported register count from 713 to 778; the table write address is\n  still the longest path.\n- No conditional-branch prediction behavior changed. The package include in\n  `core.sv` makes the bundle typedefs visible to the requested wildcard lint\n  invocation, where `core.sv` otherwise sorts before `core_pkg.sv`.\n\n## Checks\n\n- Verilator wildcard lint: passed.\n- `RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl CORE_NAME=bench bash formal/run_all.sh`: passed,\n  53 checks and 0 failures.\n- `NRET=1 make cosim TARGET=bench`: passed, both ELF runs. The unqualified\n  command initially selected the harness default `NRET=2`, which does not\n  match this core's `nret: 1` interface.\n- `make timing TARGET=bench`: placement option 0 reported 42.404 MHz, 24\n  logic levels, 2843 logic cells, 778 registers, and 8 LUT-RAMs. The longest\n  path ends at the JALR target table's write address. This remains a timing\n  concern; only placement option 0 was run, not the median fitness flow.\n\nNo test files were changed. The main implementation concern is the extra\nfetch lookup and delayed table update's impact on Fmax and registers. CoreMark\ncosim correctness passed, but the timing result needs comparison in the full\nfitness flow.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_49","type":"agent_message","text":"Implemented the 16-entry tagged JALR target buffer. EX redirects on misses or target mismatches; predicted misaligned targets return fetch to `pc + 4` while preserving the existing trap behavior. Training uses registered EX/MEM results.\n\n**Checks:** wildcard Verilator lint passed; formal passed (53 checks, 0 failures); cosim passed both ELF runs with `NRET=1`.\n\nTiming option 0 reported **42.404 MHz**. The predictor table write address remains the longest path, so Fmax is a concern; the median fitness flow was not run. Details are in [implementation_notes.md](/srv/hwebench/clones/gpt-6-luna_xhigh-v2-rep1/cores/bench/worktrees/hyp-20260929-003-r3s2/cores/bench/implementation_notes.md)."}}
