{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_3", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'tail -20 cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'", "aggregated_output": "{\"id\": \"hyp-20260929-003-r4s2\", \"title\": \"Small return address stack predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"Function returns use JALR and currently redirect from EX on every execution. The prior PC-tagged target buffer reduced Fmax and could not distinguish the same return instruction reached through different callers. A return address stack represents the call nesting directly, so it can recover the right caller-specific target with a small target array and without per-PC tag comparisons.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Function returns use JALR and currently redirect from EX on every execution. The prior PC-tagged target buffer reduced Fmax and could not distinguish the same return instruction reached through different callers. A return address stack represents the call nesting directly, so it can recover the right caller-specific target with a small target array and without per-PC tag comparisons.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add an eight-entry return address stack for standard RISC-V link-register calls and returns. Fetch predicts a JALR return (rd=x0, rs1=x1 or x5) to the aligned stack top when available, carries that predicted target with the instruction, and EX checks it against the architectural successor, redirecting on any mismatch (including a misaligned-target trap that must resume linearly). EX updates the stack on resolved, non-trapping calls that write x1/x5 and pops on resolved, non-trapping returns, so shared return sites work across callers. This narrowly targets recurring function-return bubbles while keeping all other JALR behavior on the existing redirect path.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Carry a predicted-target-valid bit and target through the fetch and ID/EX bundles.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add an eight-entry return address stack, identify return JALRs from instruction fields, and select an aligned stack-top target on a hit.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"descri\n…[70425 of 73497 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:9f24e49881a6d984]…\n opcode (or source-use qualifiers) to the load-use hazard logic.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Gate rs1 and rs2 load-use comparisons according to whether the consumer instruction actually reads each source register.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 112.23, \"delta_pct\": -6.04, \"vs_baseline\": 825.99, \"fmax_mhz\": 47.909, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4268850, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 3183, \"ff\": 697, \"seeds\": [48.562, 47.909, 47.909], \"perturbations\": [[0, 48.562, 19], [1, 47.909, 14], [2, 47.909, 14]], \"lutram\": 40, \"bsram\": 6, \"dsp\": 6, \"critical_path\": {\"from\": \"cpu/u_rf/regs_regs_0_0_s\", \"to\": \"cpu/u_id/id_ex_w.rs1_val_19_s1\", \"slack_ns\": -15.592, \"arrival_ns\": 25.886, \"logic_cells\": 38, \"modules\": [\"clock_ibuf\", \"cpu/u_id\", \"cpu/u_ex\", \"cpu/u_if\", \"cpu/u_id\", \"cpu/u_if\", \"cpu/u_hazard\", \"cpu/u_id\"]}, \"logic_levels\": 19, \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-29T15:22:43.748080Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 11}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
