om before adding it.\n- 2026-09-29 hyp-20260929-001-r4s0 (regression, -3.29%): Replacing variable register-file indexing with a decoded masked-OR tree did not guarantee a shallower mapped read path; decode fanout and reduction cost can outweigh the intended mux-depth gain.\n- 2026-09-29 hyp-20260929-003-r4s2 (regression, -5.05%): Even a caller-aware return stack can lose on CoreMark when same-cycle return decoding, stack-top access, and target selection deepen the fetch-to-PC path beyond the bubbles it removes.\n- 2026-09-29 hyp-20260929-001-r5s0 (regression, -5.75%): Sharing native wide branch predicates helped the redirect cone, but manually splitting the 32-bit comparison into byte compares and equality-qualified combines regressed, showing combine logic can outweigh local comparator depth savings.\n- 2026-09-29 hyp-20260929-002-r5s1 (regression, -19.32%): Resolving branches in ID without ID/EX and EX/MEM bypasses makes dependent branches wait for producers to reach WB, adding enough interlock cycles on CoreMark to outweigh the shorter redirect path.\n- 2026-09-29 hyp-20260929-003-r5s2 (regression, +2.55%): A tagged BHT with a backward-taken miss fallback still regressed CoreMark when IF combined tag lookup, branch-immediate target generation, and next-PC selection in one fetch path, so branch-accuracy gains must outweigh the added fetch-to-PC logic.\n- 2026-09-29 hyp-20260929-001-r6s0 (regression, +3.40%): Retiming the register-file WB bypass into EX traded the late ID mux for a shadow-WB compare and wider operand mux on the branch-redirect path, so moving bypass selection can worsen timing when EX operands also drive redirects.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
