{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cat > cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260929-001-r11s0.yaml <<'EOF'\nid: hyp-20260929-001-r11s0\ntitle: \\\"Qualify load-use stalls by source usage\\\"\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The load-use interlock currently compares a preceding load's destination against both register-index fields of every following instruction. For OP-IMM, LOAD, and JALR instructions, bits [24:20] are immediate bits rather than an rs2 source, so accidental matches can insert unnecessary bubbles in CoreMark. The recent forwarding and redirect-path experiments suggest a narrow IPC optimization is preferable to adding another wide datapath or control stage.\nhypothesis: |\n  Qualify the load-use comparisons with the consumer opcode's actual source usage: compare rs1 for R-type, OP-IMM, LOAD, STORE, BRANCH, and JALR instructions; compare rs2 only for R-type, STORE, and BRANCH instructions. Keep the existing nonzero-destination check and all bus-stall, divider, redirect, and flush priorities intact. This removes false load-use stalls when immediate fields happen to match a load destination, improving CoreMark instructions per cycle with only small opcode-decode logic.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 5\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \\\"Pass the IF/ID instruction opcode (or source-use qualifiers) to the load-use hazard logic.\\\"\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: \\\"Gate rs1 and rs2 load-use comparisons according to whether the consumer instruction actually reads each source register.\\\"\nEOF\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
