{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_3", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'ls -la cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses && cat cores/bench/rtl/hazard_unit.sv && cat cores/bench/rtl/reg_file.sv'", "aggregated_output": "total 16\ndrwxrwx---+ 2 nobody nogroup 4096 Sep 29 14:53 .\ndrwxrwx---+ 3 nobody nogroup 4096 Sep 29 14:53 ..\n// rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n//\n// Detects the only data hazard the textbook 5-stage doesn't cover via\n// forwarding: load-use. A LOAD in EX produces its data only after MEM,\n// so an instruction immediately behind that consumes the LOAD's rd\n// must be stalled by exactly one cycle.\n//\n// Outputs:\n//   stall_if / stall_id : freeze the PC reg and the IF/ID combinational\n//                          payload (cleared by ID's flush input).\n//   flush_if / flush_id : on EX redirect, kill the two in-flight\n//                          instructions ahead of the redirect target.\n//   flush_id            : also kills ID's own register on load-use to\n//                          inject a single-cycle bubble between LOAD\n//                          and the dependent instruction.\n//\n// Latency:        combinational.\n// RVFI fields:    n/a (governs validity of subsequent retirements).\nmodule hazard_unit (\n  input  logic       id_ex_mem_read,    // ID/EX.ctrl.mem_read (LOAD in EX)\n  input  logic [4:0] id_ex_rd,          // ID/EX.rd            (LOAD's dest)\n  input  logic [4:0] if_id_rs1,         // IF/ID instr[19:15]  (next rs1)\n  input  logic [4:0] if_id_rs2,         // IF/ID instr[24:20]  (next rs2)\n  input  logic       redirect,          // EX has resolved a branch/jump\n  input  logic       div_stall,         // iterative DIV/REM is still in EX\n  // Bus backpressure (default-1 in zero-wait testbenches; VexRiscv-style\n  // random ~22% stall in vex_main.cpp). When low, the corresponding\n  // memory request is NOT serviced this cycle.\n  input  logic       imem_ready,\n  input  logic       dmem_ready,\n  // EX/MEM has a memory op in flight (the LOAD/STORE the dmem stall\n  // would actually be holding up). Computed at top level from the\n  // EX/MEM register's ctrl.mem_read | ctrl.mem_write.\n  input  logic       ex_mem_mem_op,\n  output logic       stall_if,          // PC reg holds\n  output logic       stall_id,          // ID/EX regis\n…[2893 of 5965 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:303f49235963ff0d]…\n(phase 5).\n//\n// Latency:        write = 1 cycle (synchronous), read = combinational.\n// RVFI fields:    feeds rs1_rdata, rs2_rdata, rd_wdata.\nmodule reg_file (\n  input  logic        clock,\n  input  logic        reset,\n\n  input  logic [4:0]  rs1_addr,\n  input  logic [4:0]  rs2_addr,\n  output logic [31:0] rs1_data,\n  output logic [31:0] rs2_data,\n\n  input  logic        w_en,\n  input  logic [4:0]  w_addr,\n  input  logic [31:0] w_data\n);\n\n  logic [31:0] regs [0:31];\n\n  always_ff @(posedge clock) begin\n    if (reset) begin\n      for (int i = 0; i < 32; i++) regs[i] <= 32'b0;\n    end else if (w_en && w_addr != 5'b0) begin\n      regs[w_addr] <= w_data;\n    end\n  end\n\n  always_comb begin\n    if (rs1_addr == 5'b0)\n      rs1_data = 32'b0;\n    else if (w_en && w_addr == rs1_addr)\n      rs1_data = w_data;\n    else\n      rs1_data = regs[rs1_addr];\n\n    if (rs2_addr == 5'b0)\n      rs2_data = 32'b0;\n    else if (w_en && w_addr == rs2_addr)\n      rs2_data = w_data;\n    else\n      rs2_data = regs[rs2_addr];\n  end\n\nendmodule\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python3 -c 'import json; p=\\\"cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\\\"; rows=[json.loads(x) for x in open(p)]; [(print(\\\"\\\\n\\\",r[\\\"id\\\"],r.get(\\\"category\\\"),r.get(\\\"outcome\\\"),r.get(\\\"delta_pct\\\"),r.get(\\\"title\\\")), print((r.get(\\\"hypothesis\\\") or {}).get(\\\"hypothesis\\\",\\\"\\\")[:1200])) for r in rows if r.get(\\\"category\\\")==\\\"micro_opt\\\"]'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python3 -c 'import json; p=\\\"cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\\\"; rows=[json.loads(x) for x in open(p)]; [(print(\\\"\\\\n\\\",r[\\\"id\\\"],r.get(\\\"category\\\"),r.get(\\\"outcome\\\"),r.get(\\\"delta_pct\\\"),r.get(\\\"title\\\")), print((r.get(\\\"hypothesis\\\") or {}).get(\\\"hypothesis\\\",\\\"\\\")[:1200])) for r in rows if r.get(\\\"category\\\")==\\\"micro_opt\\\"]'\"","aggregated_output":"\n baseline-bench-d64d0b9 micro_opt improvement 0.0 Baseline retest for cores/bench\n\n\n hyp-20260929-001-r1s0 micro_opt regression 2.97 One-hot forwarding operand mux\nDrive one-hot selects for the register-file, EX/MEM, and MEM/WB sources and combine masked operands with an OR in EX. This removes the encoded-select decode and serial priority muxing from both forwarded operands, shortening the path into the ALU and memory address while preserving EX/MEM-over-MEM/WB priority and x0 behavior.\n\n\n hyp-20260929-001-r2s0 micro_opt regression 3.22 Dedicated load-store address adder path\nCompute the effective address for loads and stores directly from the forwarded base and decoded immediate, then register that result into the existing EX/MEM address field for memory operations. This removes the general ALU operation-result selection from the path to dmem while keeping the same one-cycle execution and memory protocol. Other instructions continue to use the existing ALU result, and the effective address remains the same value reported and consumed by MEM.\n\n\n hyp-20260929-001-r3s0 micro_opt improvement 7.46 Share branch comparison logic\nRewrite EX branch condition generation around one unsigned less-than comparison and one equality comparison. Derive signed less-than from the unsigned result and operand sign bits, and implement the greater-or-equal cases as complements of the corresponding less-than result. Selecting among these shared predicates should reduce comparator logic and the branch-to-hazard control cone without changing redirect timing, branch semantics, or the pipeline.\n\n\n hyp-20260929-001-r4s0 micro_opt regression -3.29 One-hot register-file read selection\nReplace each variable-index register-file read with a decoded one-hot select and a balanced masked-OR reduction across the 32 words. Keep x0 reads at zero, preserve the same-cycle write-first bypass, and leave the synchronous write and reset behavior unchanged. The flatter read network should shorten the register-file-to-operand path feeding the existing branch/hazard loop, improving Fmax without changing pipeline latency or instruction behavior.\n\n\n hyp-20260929-001-r5s0 micro_opt regression -5.75 Parallelize branch comparison decisions\nRestructure the EX branch comparator as parallel 8-bit unsigned comparisons with a balanced high-to-low combine, deriving signed less-than from operand signs, and express the six legal branch outcomes directly from the branch-op bits and shared equality/less-than predicates. This targets the branch-decision depth feeding redirect while preserving branch semantics, misalignment traps, and the existing pipeline and forwarding behavior.\n\n\n hyp-20260929-001-r6s0 micro_opt regression 3.4 Retiming writeback bypass into EX\nRemove the write-first bypass muxes from the register-file read ports and retain the most recent valid WB destination and value in a small shadow register. Add that value as a lower-priority EX forwarding source, behind EX/MEM and MEM/WB, so an instruction that captured stale register-file data on the WB edge receives the just-written value when it executes. Hold the shadow entry while the ID/EX instruction is stalled, so an older WB write cannot evict the bypass needed when that instruction resumes. This retimes the WB-to-consumer correction from the RF read cone into the existing EX forwarding network while keeping instruction results and retirement operands unchanged.\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
