{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,120p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260929-002-r11s1.yaml\"","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20260929-002-r11s1\ntitle: \"Buffer blocked stores to overlap pipeline work\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  A data-memory wait currently freezes EX/MEM and the stages behind it for every stalled load or store. Stores do not need a returned value, so a waiting store can be retained independently while already ordered non-memory instructions continue through the pipeline.\nhypothesis: |\n  Add a one-entry store buffer that captures a valid, aligned store when the data bus is not ready. Keep its address, byte mask, and write data stable on the memory interface until accepted, and let the store retire when the request is safely buffered. Allow younger non-memory instructions to advance while the buffer drains, but block every younger load or store until it is empty. This should hide part of the randomized store-wait latency without allowing memory operations to pass one another or changing the core's RVFI interface.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 7\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Integrate buffered-store occupancy and acceptance into the pipeline's data-wait control while preserving in-order retirement.\"\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: \"Capture stalled store requests in a one-entry buffer and drive the pending request with stable address, mask, and data until io_dmemReady.\"\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: \"Permit non-memory instructions to advance behind a buffered store and hold younger memory operations until the store drains.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"agent_message","text":"Wrote the structural hypothesis to [hyp-20260929-002-r11s1.yaml](/srv/hwebench/clones/gpt-6-luna_xhigh-v2-rep1/cores/bench/worktrees/hyp-20260929-002-r11s1-hypgen/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260929-002-r11s1.yaml).\n\nIt proposes a one-entry store buffer so non-memory instructions can advance during a stalled store, while younger memory operations wait for the buffer to drain."}}
