{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"mkdir -p cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses && cat > cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260929-002-r12s1.yaml <<'EOF'\nid: hyp-20260929-002-r12s1\ntitle: \\\"Bank the register file read ports\\\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The current register file presents two asynchronous 32-entry indexed reads, and recent timing paths pass from ID operand selection into EX arithmetic. A hierarchical banked read topology may reduce address fanout and mapped mux depth on those operand paths while keeping the existing in-order pipeline and bypass rules intact.\nhypothesis: |\n  Restructure the 32 x 32 register file as four 8-register banks. Read each bank using the low address bits, then select among the four bank outputs using the high address bits. Decode writes into the selected bank and preserve x0 hardwiring, synchronous writes, reset behavior, and same-cycle WB write-through on both read ports. The smaller local read muxes and reduced global address fanout should improve the register-file-to-EX timing path without adding pipeline latency or changing hazards and retirement.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/reg_file.sv\n    description: \\\"Implement four 8-register banks with hierarchical two-port reads while preserving write, reset, x0, and WB bypass semantics.\\\"\nEOF\n\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
