{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"agent_message","text":"The strongest structural lead is to retime only conditional-branch recovery. The prior recovery-register experiment delayed JAL/JALR too, while conditional branches are usually predicted correctly; keeping jumps immediate should preserve their throughput and cut the branch compare-to-fetch feedback path."}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"mkdir -p cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses && cat > cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260929-002-r14s1.yaml <<'EOF'\nid: hyp-20260929-002-r14s1\ntitle: \\\"Retiming conditional branch recovery\\\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  Recent timing paths cross EX, IF, hazard control, and decode, so conditional branch resolution can feed fetch redirection and younger-token flushing within one cycle. The earlier registered-recovery experiment delayed jumps as well as branches and regressed; conditional branches are often predicted correctly by the existing backward and loop prediction, making them a narrower place to add a pipeline boundary.\nhypothesis: |\n  Capture a conditional branch's recovery-valid bit and resolved next PC in the existing EX/MEM boundary, and apply recovery in the following cycle. Preserve same-cycle JAL/JALR redirects. On recovery, flush the fetched and decoded younger tokens and squash the current EX token so a wrong-path jump or memory operation cannot escape. Correctly predicted branches incur no recovery bubble, while separating branch comparison from the fetch-PC and hazard-control feedback path should improve Fmax with recovery cost limited to mispredictions.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: \\\"Add a conditional-branch recovery marker to the EX/MEM payload so misprediction state crosses the existing pipeline boundary.\\\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \\\"Combine delayed branch recovery with immediate jump redirects, prioritize the older recovery target, and wire squash control into EX.\\\"\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: \\\"Register branch recovery with the resolved next PC, retain immediate JAL/JALR redirects, and discard the current EX token during older branch recovery.\\\"\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: \\\"Flush fetch and decode on delayed branch recovery while preserving existing stall priorities.\\\"\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: \\\"Apply the registered branch recovery target to the PC while retaining prediction and stall behavior.\\\"\nEOF\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
