{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260929-003-r12s2.yaml'","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20260929-003-r12s2\ntitle: \"Disable exit prediction for variable-trip loops\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The four-entry loop predictor is the strongest measured predictor, but a learned trip count can become stale when a loop later runs for a different number of iterations. Predicting exit at that stale count adds a redirect, while enabling prediction after only one sample already regressed. The predictor should keep its multi-sample confidence rule and recognize loops whose observed trip counts vary.\nhypothesis: |\n  Add one sticky variability bit per loop-table entry. When a completed invocation has a different trip count from an established learned count, set the bit; gate learned loop-exit predictions on that bit being clear. Clear it only when the entry is replaced by a different branch PC. Stable loops retain the existing two-matching-observation policy, while branches with varying trip counts fall back to backward-taken prediction instead of repeatedly applying stale exit counts. This may avoid extra redirects on variable-length loops without increasing table capacity or widening the fetch lookup.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: \"Track sticky trip-count variability for each loop-table entry and disable learned exit prediction after an established entry observes a different trip count.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
