{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260929-003-r1s2.yaml'","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20260929-003-r1s2\ntitle: \"Static backward-taken branch prediction\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  Taken control transfers currently redirect in EX and flush younger work, costing fetch slots on common loop backedges. CoreMark spends substantial time in counted loops, so predicting backward branches in fetch can remove repeated redirect bubbles with little predictor state.\nhypothesis: |\n  Add a static fetch-stage predictor that predicts aligned JAL instructions and backward conditional branches as taken, while predicting forward branches and JALR as not taken. Carry the prediction bit with the instruction and redirect from EX only when the resolved outcome disagrees. This should reduce CoreMark loop-control bubbles without a table lookup or predictor RAM on the fetch path.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 7\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: \"Carry the fetch prediction bit through the IF/ID and ID/EX pipeline bundles.\"\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: \"Predecode direct branch and JAL targets, predict aligned JALs and backward branches taken, and select the predicted next fetch PC.\"\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: \"Capture the prediction metadata alongside the instruction in ID/EX.\"\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: \"Compare actual control-flow outcome with the carried prediction and redirect only on a misprediction, preserving misalignment traps.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
