{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cat cores/bench/LESSONS.md && jq -r 'select(.category == \\\"predictor\\\") | [.id, .title, .outcome, .delta_pct, .critical_path.from, .critical_path.to, (.lesson // \\\"\\\")] | @tsv' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"","aggregated_output":"- 2026-09-29 hyp-20260929-002-r1s1 (regression, -12.87%): Registering operands before the ALU leaves the immediately preceding ALU result outside the operand-stage bypass network, so RAW-heavy CoreMark code must interlock and can lose more throughput than the shorter critical path gains.\n- 2026-09-29 hyp-20260929-001-r2s0 (regression, +3.22%): A dedicated EX effective-address adder and EX/MEM address register can remove dmem from the critical path, but if the ALU result path becomes the new limiter, the extra 32-bit register bank raises area without improving Fmax.\n- 2026-09-29 hyp-20260929-002-r2s1 (improvement, +737.71%): Moving DIV/REM out of the combinational ALU into a shared iterative EX divider sharply improved CoreMark fitness because division is rare, provided the interlock lets older work drain and snapshots forwarded operands until the divide completes.\n- 2026-09-29 hyp-20260929-003-r2s2 (regression, +3.80%): A 32-entry BHT indexed by PC[6:2] scored below static backward-taken on CoreMark; low-bit aliasing and weakly-taken cold starts can erase adaptivity gains, so verify per-branch accuracy before replacing a simple direction rule.\n- 2026-09-29 hyp-20260929-001-r3s0 (improvement, +7.46%): Sharing EX branch equality and unsigned less-than predicates, with signed order derived from operand signs, improved fitness while the redirect/hazard loop remained critical, showing comparator sharing can trim this cone without removing its control-path bottleneck.\n- 2026-09-29 hyp-20260929-003-r3s2 (regression, -5.02%): A PC-tagged JALR target buffer can mispredict return sites reached from multiple callers, while its opcode/tag lookup and target mux add fetch-to-PC timing pressure, so measure target reuse and timing headroom before adding it.\n- 2026-09-29 hyp-20260929-001-r4s0 (regression, -3.29%): Replacing variable register-file indexing with a decoded masked-OR tree did not guarantee a shallower mapped read path; decode fanout and reduction cost can outweigh the intended mux-depth gain.\n- 2026-09-29 hyp-20260929-003-r4s2 (regression, -5.05%): Even a caller-aware return stack can lose on CoreMark when same-cycle return decoding, stack-top access, and target selection deepen the fetch-to-PC path beyond the bubbles it removes.\nhyp-20260929-003-r1s2\tStatic backward-taken branch prediction\tregression\t4.54\tcpu/u_mem/reg_q.rd_2_s0\tdmem_3_dmem_3_0_0_s\t\nhyp-20260929-003-r2s2\tAdaptive bimodal branch predictor\tregression\t3.8\tcpu/u_mem/reg_q.rd_0_s0\tdmem_0_dmem_0_0_0_s\t- 2026-09-29 hyp-20260929-003-r2s2 (regression, +3.80%): A 32-entry BHT indexed by PC[6:2] scored below static backward-taken on CoreMark; low-bit aliasing and weakly-taken cold starts can erase adaptivity gains, so verify per-branch accuracy before replacing a simple direction rule.\nhyp-20260929-003-r3s2\tTagged indirect return target predictor\tregression\t-5.02\tcpu/u_rf/regs_regs_0_0_s\tcpu/u_if/jalr_target_jalr_target_0_0_s\t- 2026-09-29 hyp-20260929-003-r3s2 (regression, -5.02%): A PC-tagged JALR target buffer can mispredict return sites reached from multiple callers, while its opcode/tag lookup and target mux add fetch-to-PC timing pressure, so measure target reuse and timing headroom before adding it.\nhyp-20260929-003-r4s2\tSmall return address stack predictor\tregression\t-5.05\tcpu/u_rf/regs_regs_0_0_s\tcpu/u_id/id_ex_w.rs1_val_15_s1\t- 2026-09-29 hyp-20260929-003-r4s2 (regression, -5.05%): Even a caller-aware return stack can lose on CoreMark when same-cycle return decoding, stack-top access, and target selection deepen the fetch-to-PC path beyond the bubbles it removes.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"comp