{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"jq -r 'select(.category == \\\"predictor\\\") | \\\"ID: \\\" + .id + \\\"\\\\n\\\" + .title + \\\"\\\\n\\\" + .hypothesis.hypothesis + \\\"\\\\nNotes: \\\" + (.implementation_notes // \\\"\\\") + \\\"\\\\nDelta: \\\" + (.delta_pct|tostring) + \\\"\\\\n\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"","aggregated_output":"ID: hyp-20260929-003-r1s2\nStatic backward-taken branch prediction\nAdd a static fetch-stage predictor that predicts aligned JAL instructions and backward conditional branches as taken, while predicting forward branches and JALR as not taken. Carry the prediction bit with the instruction and redirect from EX only when the resolved outcome disagrees. This should reduce CoreMark loop-control bubbles without a table lookup or predictor RAM on the fetch path.\n\nNotes: \nDelta: 4.54\n\nID: hyp-20260929-003-r2s2\nAdaptive bimodal branch predictor\nAdd a 32-entry, two-bit saturating counter predictor indexed by the conditional branch PC, initialized weakly taken. Predict direct JALs taken and JALRs not taken as before, carry the predicted direction to EX, and update the counter from each resolved conditional branch. EX redirects only when the resolved direction differs from the prediction; fetch computes the direct branch target in parallel and suppresses taken predictions for misaligned targets. This should improve CoreMark IPC over the static rule with modest state and no table lookup in the EX critical path.\n\nNotes: \nDelta: 3.8\n\nID: hyp-20260929-003-r3s2\nTagged indirect return target predictor\nAdd a 16-entry direct-mapped JALR target buffer indexed by the fetch PC, with a full PC tag, valid bit, and aligned target. Predict a JALR taken only on a tag hit; carry its predicted target with the instruction and have EX redirect only when the target was absent or differs from the resolved target. Train the entry from resolved JALR instructions in EX. This should accelerate recurring function returns while keeping first-use and alias misses precise, avoiding speculative return-stack state, and leaving conditional-branch prediction unchanged.\n\nNotes: \nDelta: -5.02\n\nID: hyp-20260929-003-r4s2\nSmall return address stack predictor\nAdd an eight-entry return address stack for standard RISC-V link-register calls and returns. Fetch predicts a JALR return (rd=x0, rs1=x1 or x5) to the aligned stack top when available, carries that predicted target with the instruction, and EX checks it against the architectural successor, redirecting on any mismatch (including a misaligned-target trap that must resume linearly). EX updates the stack on resolved, non-trapping calls that write x1/x5 and pops on resolved, non-trapping returns, so shared return sites work across callers. This narrowly targets recurring function-return bubbles while keeping all other JALR behavior on the existing redirect path.\n\nNotes: \nDelta: -5.05\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"agent_message","text":"The predictor history points to one defensible angle: the static backward-taken rule nearly cleared the acceptance bar, while the untagged table and indirect-target schemes lost. I’m drafting a small tagged direction table that retains the static rule on misses and avoids indirect target lookup."}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cat > cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260929-003-r5s2.yaml <<'EOF'\nid: hyp-20260929-003-r5s2\ntitle: \\\"Tagged loop direction predictor\\\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  Static backward-taken prediction nearly met the fitness acceptance threshold, but it cannot adapt at loop exits or to forward-taken branches. The prior bimodal table was indexed by low PC bits, so unrelated branches aliased and erased some of that gain. A small tagged table can learn hot branch behavior while preserving the useful static default when an entry is absent or displaced.\nhypothesis: |\n  Add a 16-entry direct-mapped conditional-branch direction table indexed by PC[5:2], with a full upper-PC tag and a two-bit saturating direction counter. On a tag hit, predict from the counter; on a miss, retain the existing backward-taken rule. Update the matching entry from each resolved conditional branch in EX, and carry its prediction with the instruction so EX redirects only on a direction mismatch. Keep direct branch target generation and all JAL/JALR handling unchanged. Tag checks avoid low-bit alias pollution while the miss fallback preserves the proven loop bias, improving CoreMark branch accuracy with modest state and no indirect-target lookup.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 7\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: Carry the conditional-branch prediction bit through the fetch, decode, and execute bundles.\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: Add the 16-entry tagged direction table, use backward-taken prediction on misses, and select the direct target for predicted-taken branches.\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: Preserve the fetch prediction metadata in ID/EX.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: Compare resolved branch direction with its prediction and expose the resolved branch outcome for table training while preserving misalignment trap behavior.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: Connect resolved conditional-branch updates from EX to the fetch predictor.\nEOF\n\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
