{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_1", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg -n -i 'predictor|BHT|branch|return stack|static backward|JALR' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"", "aggregated_output": "3:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260929-002-r1s1\", \"title\": \"Split execute into operand and ALU stages\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The measured critical path starts at the MEM/WB destination register, passes through EX forwarding and the ALU, and ends at the data-memory address. This combines register matching, operand selection, and 32-bit execute arithmetic in one cycle, limiting Fmax.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The measured critical path starts at the MEM/WB destination register, passes through EX forwarding and the ALU, and ends at the data-memory address. This combines register matching, operand selection, and 32-bit execute arithmetic in one cycle, limiting Fmax.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Split EX into a forwarding/operand stage and a registered ALU/branch stage. This puts a pipeline boundary between the forwarding muxes and the main execute arithmetic, shortening the longest combinational path while retaining one-instruction-per-cycle throughput when unstalled. Extend the forwarding, interlock, redirect-flush, and memory-stall control for the extra stage, and keep the resulting retirement metadata aligned so RVFI and memory behavior remain unchanged.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 8, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the additional execute pipeline stage and preserve retirement and memory interfaces.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Define the registered operand-stage bundle and carry all instruction metadata across the new boundary.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Split forwarding/operand capture from ALU execution and branch resolution across two stages.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/forward_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Adapt producer priority and bypass selection for the added execute stage.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Update load-use interlocks, redirect flushing, and backpressure holds for the deeper pipeline.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 8, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl\n…[38608 of 41680 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:b8e753d7bfd154d1]…\nct\": 2.55, \"vs_baseline\": 823.1, \"fmax_mhz\": 47.521, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4247330, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 2994, \"ff\": 717, \"seeds\": [46.746, 47.521, 47.521], \"perturbations\": [[0, 46.746, 22], [1, 47.521, 21], [2, 47.521, 21]], \"lutram\": 22, \"bsram\": 6, \"dsp\": 6, \"critical_path\": {\"from\": \"cpu/u_rf/regs_regs_0_0_s\", \"to\": \"cpu/u_id/id_ex_w.rs1_val_0_s100\", \"slack_ns\": -16.392, \"arrival_ns\": 26.687, \"logic_cells\": 47, \"modules\": [\"clock_ibuf\", \"cpu/u_id\", \"cpu/u_ex\", \"cpu/u_if\", \"cpu/u_hazard\", \"cpu/u_id\"]}, \"logic_levels\": 22, \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-29T12:27:00.868151Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 5, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-29 hyp-20260929-003-r5s2 (regression, +2.55%): A tagged BHT with a backward-taken miss fallback still regressed CoreMark when IF combined tag lookup, branch-immediate target generation, and next-PC selection in one fetch path, so branch-accuracy gains must outweigh the added fetch-to-PC logic.\"}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\np='cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'\nfor line in open(p):\n d=json.loads(line)\n if d.get('category')=='predictor':\n  print('\\\\n',d.get('id'),d.get('title'),'outcome=',d.get('outcome'),'fitness=',d.get('fitness'),'delta=',d.get('delta_pct'),'fmax=',d.get('fmax_mhz'),'cycles=',d.get('cycles'))\n  hyp=d.get('hypothesis',{})\n  print('HYP:',hyp.get('hypothesis','').replace('\\\\n',' '))\n  print('PATH:',d.get('critical_path',{}).get('modules'))\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_2", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\np='cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'\nfor line in open(p):\n d=json.loads(line)\n if d.get('category')=='predictor':\n  print('\\\\n',d.get('id'),d.get('title'),'outcome=',d.get('outcome'),'fitness=',d.get('fitness'),'delta=',d.get('delta_pct'),'fmax=',d.get('fmax_mhz'),'cycles=',d.get('cycles'))\n  hyp=d.get('hypothesis',{})\n  print('HYP:',hyp.get('hypothesis','').replace('\\\\n',' '))\n  print('PATH:',d.get('critical_path',{}).get('modules'))\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "\n hyp-20260929-003-r1s2 Static backward-taken branch prediction outcome= regression fitness= 12.67 delta= 4.54 fmax= 5.399 cycles= 4259699\nHYP: Add a static fetch-stage predictor that predicts aligned JAL instructions and backward conditional branches as taken, while predicting forward branches and JALR as not taken. Carry the prediction bit with the instruction and redirect from EX only when the resolved outcome disagrees. This should reduce CoreMark loop-control bubbles without a table lookup or predictor RAM on the fetch path. \nPATH: ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_fwd', 'cpu/u_ex', 'cpu/u_ex/u_alu', 'cpu/u_ex']\n\n hyp-20260929-003-r2s2 Adaptive bimodal branch predictor outcome= regression fitness= 12.58 delta= 3.8 fmax= 5.351 cycles= 4253884\nHYP: Add a 32-entry, two-bit saturating counter predictor indexed by the conditional branch PC, initialized weakly taken. Predict direct JALs taken and JALRs not taken as before, carry the predicted direction to EX, and update the counter from each resolved conditional branch. EX redirects only when the resolved direction differs from the prediction; fetch computes the direct branch target in parallel and suppresses taken predictions for misaligned targets. This should improve CoreMark IPC over the static rule with modest state and no table lookup in the EX critical path. \nPATH: ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_fwd', 'cpu/u_ex', 'cpu/u_ex/u_alu', 'cpu/u_ex']\n\n hyp-20260929-003-r3s2 Tagged indirect return target predictor outcome= regression fitness= 96.43 delta= -5.02 fmax= 43.208 cycles= 4480979\nHYP: Add a 16-entry direct-mapped JALR target buffer indexed by the fetch PC, with a full PC tag, valid bit, and aligned target. Predict a JALR taken only on a tag hit; carry its predicted target with the instruction and have EX redirect only when the target was absent or differs from the resolved target. Train the entry from resolved JALR instructions in EX. This should accelerate recurring function returns while keeping first-use and alias misses precise, avoiding speculative return-stack state, and leaving conditional-branch prediction unchanged. \nPATH: ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_id', 'cpu/u_ex', 'cpu/u_id', 'cpu/u_if', 'cpu/u_hazard', 'cpu/u_id', 'cpu/u_if']\n\n hyp-20260929-003-r4s2 Small return address stack predictor outcome= regression fitness= 103.59 delta= -5.05 fmax= 46.388 cycles= 4478102\nHYP: Add an eight-entry return address stack for standard RISC-V link-register calls and returns. Fetch predicts a JALR return (rd=x0, rs1=x1 or x5) to the aligned stack top when available, carries that predicted target with the instruction, and EX checks it against the architectural successor, redirecting on any mismatch (including a misaligned-target trap that must resume linearly). EX updates the stack on resolved, non-trapping calls that write x1/x5 and pops on resolved, non-trapping returns, so shared return sites work across callers. This narrowly targets recurring function-return bubbles while keeping all other JALR behavior on the existing redirect path. \nPATH: ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_id', 'cpu/u_ex', 'cpu/u_id', 'cpu/u_if', 'cpu/u_hazard', 'cpu/u_id']\n\n hyp-20260929-003-r5s2 Tagged loop direction predictor outcome= regression fitness= 111.88 delta= 2.55 fmax= 47.521 cycles= 4247330\nHYP: Add a 16-entry direct-mapped conditional-branch direction table indexed by PC[5:2], with a full upper-PC tag and a two-bit saturating direction counter. On a tag hit, predict from the counter; on a miss, retain the existing backward-taken rule. Update the matching entry from each resolved conditional branch in EX, and carry its prediction with the instruction so EX redirects only on a direction mismatch. Keep direct branch target generation and all JAL/JALR handling unchanged. Tag checks avoid low-bit alias pollution while the miss fallback preserves the proven loop bias, improving CoreMark branch accuracy with modest state and no indirect-target lookup. \nPATH: ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_id', 'cpu/u_ex', 'cpu/u_if', 'cpu/u_hazard', 'cpu/u_id']\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"agent_message","text":"The useful signal is that static backward-taken prediction came closest to the acceptance bar, while direction tables and return predictors lost ground. I’m choosing a compact loop-exit predictor layered on that static rule: it targets the repeated final-iteration miss while keeping the common backward-loop prediction as the fallback."}}
