apture from ALU execution and branch resolution across two stages.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/forward_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Adapt producer priority and bypass selection for the added execute stage.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Update load-use interlocks, redirect flushing, and backpressure holds for the deeper pipeline.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 8, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl\n…[61347 of 64419 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:ff402b761c82fc9d]…\nrect path, so moving bypass selection can worsen timing when EX operands also drive redirects.\n- 2026-09-29 hyp-20260929-003-r6s2 (improvement, +9.48%): A four-entry PC-tagged loop table improved CoreMark fitness by learning taken-iteration counts to predict repeated loop exits, showing that targeted loop termination state can outperform a broader direction lookup while retaining backward-taken prediction for cold loops.\n- 2026-09-29 hyp-20260929-002-r7s1 (regression, -9.90%): Registering EX recovery adds a recovery bubble to every JAL/JALR and each branch misprediction; despite loop prediction avoiding common loop redirects, CoreMark's control transfers made that throughput cost outweigh the shorter EX-to-fetch path.\n- 2026-09-29 hyp-20260929-003-r7s2 (regression, -8.96%): Expanding the tagged loop table from four to eight entries regressed CoreMark, suggesting the extra IF-stage table state and PC[4] lookup cost more than the conflict reduction was worth; measure aliasing before scaling predictor capacity.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
