ect only when the target was absent or differs from the resolved target. Train the entry from resolved JALR instructions in EX. This should accelerate recurring function returns while keeping first-use and alias misses precise, avoiding speculative return-stack state, and leaving conditional-branch prediction unchanged.\nhyp-20260929-003-r4s2 regression -5.05 103.59 46.388 Small return address stack predictor \n Add an eight-entry return address stack for standard RISC-V link-register calls and returns. Fetch predicts a JALR return (rd=x0, rs1=x1 or x5) to the aligned stack top\n…[2206 of 5278 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:85054958ba474851]…\niss fallback. The extra capacity should reduce conflicts among hot CoreMark loop branches and preserve more learned exit predictions, improving IPC without adding another predictor structure or changing branch resolution. The fetch lookup gains one index bit, so limit the change to table capacity and keep all prediction and training behavior otherwise identical.\nhyp-20260929-003-r8s2 regression -12.63 104.35 44.547 Trim redundant loop predictor tag bits \n Store only PC[31:4] in each loop predictor tag and compare that slice during fetch and training; keep the four-entry PC[3:2] index, trip counters, confidence policy, and backward-taken fallback unchanged. Since the omitted PC[3:2] bits are fixed by the selected entry and PC[1:0] are zero for every instruction, this preserves tag match behavior while narrowing the tag registers and comparator. The smaller table and equality check should ease the fetch prediction path and improve Fmax without changing CoreMark's prediction accuracy or adding redirect bubbles.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
